{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Issachar, etc.</b> Its saying <b>חֲמוֹר גָּרֶם</b> ('a donkey caused it') means — the emergence of his mother through the donkey's being driven away (Niddah 31a). This was the reason that Issachar crouches between the saddlebags and is not permanently settled in his place. And this is the reason that men of Torah, who are the branches of Issachar, wander from city to city — for an improper act produces its like in its offspring, and Leah did something that was apparently outside the way of proper conduct; therefore that which emerges from her is found to follow this pattern. Nevertheless, they delight in the labor of Torah in pleasantness." "<b>And he saw rest, that it was good.</b> The meaning is the supernal rest — 'that it was good,' meaning: when a man gazes and contemplates all the pleasures of this sensory world — such as the craving for food and drink — the sweetness of that thing is not justified from its own side but only from the side of the body, which is an empty vessel standing to be filled; for the Fashioner implanted in it that it cannot remain empty at all. Therefore, when a man is sated, not only does he take no sweetness in that food for which his soul longed and craved at that very moment before he filled his stomach, but he loathes it as one loathes evil. And this is what Scripture says (Proverbs 23:8): ״פִּתְּךָ אָכַלְתָּ תְקִיאֶנָּה״ ('the morsel you ate you will vomit up') — meaning, the morsel of this world, if you ate it, afterward you will vomit it up, for you will loathe having brought it into your mouth. So too regarding a thing toward which a man yearns in appearance and to which his heart desires a pleasant creature — when he investigates its nature thoroughly he will see that that beauty and pleasantness is nothing but a hyyuli appearance. For when a man contemplates the flesh and the skin from which all beauty and form in a person are built, he will see and understand upon reflection that there is no pleasantness in them, and he will come to loathe human flesh at his approach, and it is all nothing but illusion. But the true good and the justified pleasantness belong to the supernal world. And therefore it says <b>and he saw rest, that it was good</b> — meaning, the world called 'rest' which is good, to exclude the good that is only imagined in this world as mentioned, <b>and the land, that it is pleasant</b> in accordance with truth." "<b>And he bent his shoulder to bear</b> the pain of this world, <b>and he became a mas</b> — meaning that he himself was a mas ('tribute') of an oved ('server'), meaning that he directed himself to serve HaShem, and made himself like a mas given, submitting himself to be an oved of the holy service that is sweet and pleasant." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in its saying <b>and he became a mas oved</b>, that all created beings will render him tribute for his sustenance and serve him as a ben Torah — just as we find regarding Issachar himself, that Zebulun served him (Genesis 49:13), and so in every generation."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:43.831507980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}